Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=52.5^\circ$. Dividing by secant multiplies by cosine because $1/\sec x=\cos x$. The resulting pair at $x=105^\circ$ permits a second application.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3\,\sin\left(105^\circ\right)}{\sec\left(105^\circ\right)}\\&=3\,\sin\left(105^\circ\right)\cos\left(105^\circ\right)\\&=\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(210^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(210^\circ\right)=- \frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{2}\,\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{3}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{16}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{8}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{12\,\sec\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{12}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{24}{\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{24}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{48}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=24 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

