Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=33.75^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=67.5^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8\,\sec\left(67.5^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(67.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(67.5^\circ\right)\cos\left(67.5^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{16}{\sin\left(135^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(135^\circ\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{16}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{32}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=16 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{12}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{6}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8\,\sec\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)\cos\left(\frac{5 \pi}{6}\right)}\\&=\frac{16}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{3}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{3}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{3}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{16}{\left(- \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{-32}{\sqrt{3}}\\&=- \frac{32 \sqrt{3}}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$
